use
  llm.reason

task doc_syntax_basics
  needs lang_name: Text
  gives Text
  do
    result = reason "You are writing technical documentation for a programming language called {lang_name}. Write a reference section about SYNTAX BASICS. Cover these exact rules with examples for each:\n\n1. Indentation: 2-space fixed levels (i1=2sp, i2=4sp, i3=6sp). No braces. Blocks are defined by indent level.\n2. Comments: # for line comments\n3. Keywords: task, pure, flow, stage, fn, needs, gives, do, give, say, use, when, else, if, match, for, in, try, or, reason, classify, search, not, and, true, false, agent, event, states, emit, transition, escalate, forward\n4. Identifiers: lowercase start, alphanumeric + underscore. Types start uppercase.\n5. Template strings: double quotes with curly brace interpolation like this format: name surrounded by curly braces inside quotes\n6. Literals: numbers (42, 3.14), booleans (true, false), arrays ([1, 2, 3])\n7. Type names: Text, Number, Bool, Results, Report, Intent, Summary, Classification\n8. Array types: Text[9] (fixed size), Player[] (dynamic)\n9. No semicolons, no braces, no parentheses around conditions\n10. Statements separated by newlines at the same indent level\n11. Blank lines NOT allowed within fn main or do blocks\n\nFor each rule provide a short code example. Format as markdown with code blocks using forge as the language tag."
    when result.sure -> give result
    when result.unsure -> give result
    else -> give "Syntax basics documentation could not be generated."

task doc_task_and_pure
  needs lang_name: Text
  gives Text
  do
    result = reason "You are writing technical documentation for {lang_name}. Write a reference section about TASK AND PURE DECLARATIONS.\n\nA task is a function that can call LLMs (oracle queries). A pure function is deterministic and the compiler forbids LLM calls inside it.\n\nTask syntax:\n  task <name>\n    needs <param>: <Type>, <param>: <Type>\n    gives <Type>\n    do\n      <statements at 4-space indent>\n\nPure syntax:\n  pure <name>\n    needs <param>: <Type>\n    gives <Type>\n    do\n      <statements at 4-space indent>\n\nKey rules:\n- task bodies can use: reason, classify, search, call other tasks\n- pure bodies CANNOT use: reason, classify, search, or call tasks (compiler error)\n- pure functions always return confidence 1.0 (deterministic)\n- give <expr> returns a value from the function (works inside if/else/match/for)\n- say <expr> prints to stdout\n- needs clause lists parameters with types\n- gives clause declares return type\n\nWorking examples:\n\n  task greet\n    needs name: Text\n    gives Text\n    do\n      give the template string Hello, with name interpolated\n\n  pure next_player\n    needs current: Text\n    gives Text\n    do\n      if current == X as a string\n        give O as a string\n      else\n        give X as a string\n\n  task analyze\n    needs text: Text\n    gives Text\n    do\n      give reason with a prompt asking to analyze the text variable\n\nProvide complete examples with correct indentation. Format as markdown."
    when result.sure -> give result
    when result.unsure -> give result
    else -> give "Task and pure documentation could not be generated."

task doc_flow_stages
  needs lang_name: Text
  gives Text
  do
    result = reason "You are writing technical documentation for {lang_name}. Write a reference section about FLOW DECLARATIONS AND PARALLEL STAGES.\n\nA flow is a multi-stage pipeline. The compiler automatically determines which stages can run in parallel based on their dependency declarations.\n\nFlow syntax:\n  flow <name>\n    needs <param>: <Type>\n    gives <Type>\n\n    stage <name>\n      <statements at 4-space indent>\n\n    stage <name>\n      needs <prev_stage>.<field>, <other_stage>.<field>\n      <statements at 4-space indent>\n\nKey rules:\n- Stages without needs clauses (or that only need flow parameters) run in Wave 1 (parallel)\n- Stages that need outputs from other stages wait for those stages to complete\n- The compiler builds a DAG and computes execution waves via topological sort\n- Stages in the same wave run concurrently (tokio tasks)\n- The last stage with a give statement produces the flow return value\n- Stage variables are exposed to dependent stages as records: stage_name.variable_name\n- needs stage.* grabs all variables from that stage\n\nWorking example:\n\n  flow review_code\n    needs code: Text\n    gives Text\n\n    stage detect\n      lang = detect_language(code)\n\n    stage quality\n      needs detect.lang\n      report = analyze_quality(code, detect.lang)\n\n    stage security\n      needs detect.lang\n      report = analyze_security(code, detect.lang)\n\n    stage verdict\n      needs detect.lang, quality.report, security.report\n      give format_verdict(detect.lang, quality.report, security.report)\n\nIn this example: detect runs first (Wave 1), then quality and security run in parallel (Wave 2, both depend only on detect), then verdict runs last (Wave 3).\n\nFormat as markdown with code blocks."
    when result.sure -> give result
    when result.unsure -> give result
    else -> give "Flow documentation could not be generated."

task doc_control_flow
  needs lang_name: Text
  gives Text
  do
    result = reason "You are writing technical documentation for {lang_name}. Write a reference section about CONTROL FLOW.\n\nFORGE has three branching constructs and one loop, each for a different purpose:\n\n1. WHEN/ELSE - Confidence-based dispatch (for LLM output uncertainty)\n   Syntax (inline, single statement per branch):\n     when <var>.sure -> <statement>\n     when <var>.unsure -> <statement>\n     when <var>.unreliable -> <statement>\n     else -> <statement>\n   Confidence thresholds: sure >= 0.8, unsure = 0.5-0.8, unreliable < 0.5\n   Custom threshold: when result.sure(above: 0.9) -> give result\n   When clauses are mutually exclusive - first match wins\n\n2. IF/ELSE IF/ELSE - Boolean branching\n   Syntax:\n     if <condition>\n       <statements at next indent>\n     else if <condition>\n       <statements>\n     else\n       <statements>\n   Conditions use: ==, !=, <, >, <=, >=, and, or, not\n\n3. MATCH - Structural pattern matching\n   Syntax:\n     match <expr>\n       <Pattern> -> <statement>\n       _ -> <statement>\n   Patterns: Wildcard (_), Constructor names (Active, Inactive), Binding variables (x)\n   Note: string literals cannot be patterns, use if/else for string comparison\n\n4. FOR - Loop over arrays\n   Syntax:\n     for <binding> in <expr>\n       <statements at next indent>\n   Iterates over Array or List values\n\nImportant: give works inside all control flow blocks - it propagates up to the enclosing function boundary.\n\nFormat as markdown with code examples for each construct."
    when result.sure -> give result
    when result.unsure -> give result
    else -> give "Control flow documentation could not be generated."

task doc_expressions
  needs lang_name: Text
  gives Text
  do
    result = reason "You are writing technical documentation for {lang_name}. Write a reference section about EXPRESSIONS AND OPERATORS.\n\nExpression types (ordered by precedence, lowest to highest):\n\n1. COMPOSE (pipe): expr >> expr >> expr\n   Evaluates left-to-right. Each step binds the previous result to _pipe variable.\n   Example: argue_for(topic) >> score_argument(_pipe)\n\n2. BOOLEAN: expr and expr, expr or expr\n\n3. COMPARISON: ==, !=, <, >, <=, >=\n   Works on Numbers and Text (Text equality is exact match)\n\n4. ARITHMETIC: +, -, *, /\n   Also: Text + Text = concatenation\n\n5. UNARY: not expr, -expr\n\n6. FAN-OUT (parallel): (expr1 | expr2 | expr3)\n   Evaluates all expressions, returns an Array with all results.\n   Example: (argue_for(topic) | argue_against(topic))\n\n7. TRY-OR (error recovery): try <expr> or <fallback>\n   If primary expression fails (any error), evaluates fallback instead.\n   Example: try undefined_task(x) or the string fallback\n\n8. LLM OPERATIONS:\n   reason <template_string> - send prompt to LLM, get Text response with confidence\n   classify <expr> into [list of string labels] - classify input into categories\n   search <template_string> - web search (currently stubbed)\n\n9. FUNCTION CALLS: task_name(arg1, arg2) or task_name(label: arg1)\n   Positional or labeled arguments supported\n\n10. POSTFIX: .field (field access), [n] (array index), .method() (method call)\n\n11. ATOMS: literals, variables, array literals [a, b, c], (parenthesized expr)\n\nFormat as markdown with examples for each operator."
    when result.sure -> give result
    when result.unsure -> give result
    else -> give "Expression documentation could not be generated."

task doc_methods_and_config
  needs lang_name: Text
  gives Text
  do
    result = reason "You are writing technical documentation for {lang_name}. Write a reference section covering TWO topics:\n\nPART 1 - BUILT-IN METHODS\nAvailable methods on values:\n- .len() / .count() - returns Number. Works on Array, List, Text (character count)\n- .contains(x) / .any(x) - returns Bool. Works on Array (element match), Text (substring)\n- .none(x) - returns Bool. True if array does NOT contain x\n- .lower() - returns Text. Converts to lowercase. Preserves confidence.\n- .upper() - returns Text. Converts to uppercase. Preserves confidence.\n- .trim() - returns Text. Strips whitespace. Preserves confidence.\n\nMethods can be chained: result.lower().contains(search_term)\nZero-arg methods require parentheses: arr.len() not arr.len\n\nPART 2 - CONFIGURATION AND PROVIDERS\nFORGE programs never name a provider. Provider is selected by config.\n\nConfig file (TOML format):\n  [llm]\n  default = provider-name\n\n  [providers.name]\n  type = anthropic or openai-compat or mock\n  model = model-name\n  api_key = can use env vars like ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in dollar-curly syntax\n  base_url = required for openai-compat\n  fallback = another-provider-name\n  timeout_secs = 120\n\nEnvironment overrides:\n  FORGE_CONFIG=path - custom config file\n  FORGE_MOCK=1 - use mock provider (deterministic, for testing)\n  FORGE_PROVIDER=name - override default provider\n\nCLI commands:\n  forge parse <file> - print AST\n  forge check <file> - type-check\n  forge run <file> - execute program\n  forge trace <file> - execute with JSON trace to stderr\n\nFormat as markdown."
    when result.sure -> give result
    when result.unsure -> give result
    else -> give "Methods and configuration documentation could not be generated."

pure section_divider
  needs title: Text
  gives Text
  do
    give "---\n\n## {title}\n"

flow generate_docs
  needs lang_name: Text
  gives Text

  stage syntax_basics
    doc = doc_syntax_basics(lang_name)

  stage task_and_pure
    doc = doc_task_and_pure(lang_name)

  stage flow_stages
    doc = doc_flow_stages(lang_name)

  stage control_flow
    doc = doc_control_flow(lang_name)

  stage expressions
    doc = doc_expressions(lang_name)

  stage methods_config
    doc = doc_methods_and_config(lang_name)

  stage assemble
    needs syntax_basics.doc, task_and_pure.doc, flow_stages.doc, control_flow.doc, expressions.doc, methods_config.doc
    header = "# FORGE Language Reference\n\nThis document is a complete reference for the FORGE programming language, designed for teaching LLM agents how to write correct FORGE code.\n\nFORGE is a language for oracle-augmented computation where LLM calls are first-class primitives, uncertainty is tracked structurally, and parallelism is automatic.\n"
    give "{header}\n## 1. Syntax Basics\n\n{syntax_basics.doc}\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tasks and Pure Functions\n\n{task_and_pure.doc}\n\n---\n\n## 3. Flows and Parallel Stages\n\n{flow_stages.doc}\n\n---\n\n## 4. Control Flow\n\n{control_flow.doc}\n\n---\n\n## 5. Expressions and Operators\n\n{expressions.doc}\n\n---\n\n## 6. Methods and Configuration\n\n{methods_config.doc}"

fn main
  result = generate_docs("FORGE")
  say result
